<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Recherla Nayakas</h1><p class="st-meta">1368 CE – 1475 CE (approx.) · capital Rachakonda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Telugu warrior rulers of Rachakonda and Devarakonda who replaced the Musunuris in Telangana and allied with the Bahmanis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Recherla family were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velama">Velama</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> commanders who had served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kakatiya-dynasty">Kakatiya dynasty</a>. In the 1360s, allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapota-nayaka">Anapota Nayaka</a> defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya Nayaka</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/musunuri-nayaks">Musunuri Nayaks</a> around 1368 and established rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachakonda">Rachakonda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devarakonda">Devarakonda</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Recherlas, also called Padmanayakas, ruled through a network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> and warrior families. Their courts supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> writing; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarvajna-singabhupala">Sarvajna Singabhupala</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasarnava-sudhakara">Rasarnava Sudhakara</a>, a treatise on poetics. They generally sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmanis</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/reddi-kingdom">Reddi Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Over the 15th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> turned against its former allies and gradually annexed their forts; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachakonda">Rachakonda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devarakonda">Devarakonda</a> were absorbed by about 1475. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velama">Velama</a> families later served as zamindars under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a> and the British.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/recherla-nayakas.jpg" alt="Recherla Nayakas" width="320" height="225"><figcaption>Image: Vinay332211 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lepakshi...jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1368 CE – 1475 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rachakonda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anapota Nayaka, Sarvajna Singabhupala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Padmanayakas, Nayakas of Rachakonda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=recherla-nayakas" aria-label="Recherla Nayakas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6822 1540 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Recherla Nayakas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7142 1796L7159 1767M7142 1796L7088 1790"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7159 1767h0M7088 1790h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7142 1796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7142 1796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rachakonda. <a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=recherla-nayakas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

